        <section id="english">

            <h2>
                Technical Explanation — English
            </h2>

            <h3>
                1. Copilot Inside the Development Environment
            </h3>

            <p>
                GitHub Copilot adds AI-assisted development capabilities
                to supported coding environments.
            </p>

            <p>
                Depending on the editor or IDE, Copilot may be available
                through an extension, integration, or built-in feature.
            </p>

            <p>
                This means the developer does not always need to leave the
                coding environment to ask an AI tool for help.
            </p>

            <h3>
                2. What Is an Extension?
            </h3>

            <p>
                An extension is an additional software component that adds
                new capabilities to an existing application.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example, Visual Studio Code can support extensions for:
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li>Programming languages.</li>
                <li>Debugging tools.</li>
                <li>Formatting.</li>
                <li>Git integration.</li>
                <li>AI coding assistance.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
                An extension does not normally replace the editor.
                Instead, it adds functionality to it.
            </p>

            <h3>
                3. Main AI Capabilities
            </h3>

            <p>
                A Copilot-enabled development environment may provide
                several forms of AI assistance.
            </p>

            <table>

                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Capability</th>
                        <th>Purpose</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>

                <tbody>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            Code Completion
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Suggests code while the developer is typing.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            Code Generation
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Generates code from an instruction or prompt.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            AI Chat
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Lets developers ask questions about code and
                            software problems.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                    <tr>
                        <td>
                            Agentic Assistance
                        </td>

                        <td>
                            Can attempt several related steps toward a
                            development goal.
                        </td>
                    </tr>

                </tbody>

            </table>

            <h3>
                4. Code Completion
            </h3>

            <p>
                Suppose you start writing:
            </p>

            <pre><code>function greetStudent(name) {</code></pre>

            <p>
                An AI coding assistant may predict the next part:
            </p>

            <pre><code>function greetStudent(name) {
    return "Welcome, " + name;
}</code></pre>

            <p>
                This is called a code suggestion or code completion.
            </p>

            <p>
                You should still understand the suggestion before
                accepting it.
            </p>

            <h3>
                5. Code Generation
            </h3>

            <p>
                Instead of starting the code yourself, you can also
                describe what you need.
            </p>

            <p>
                Example prompt:
            </p>

            <pre><code>Create a JavaScript function that checks
whether a quiz score is 70 or higher.</code></pre>

            <p>
                Possible generated code:
            </p>

            <pre><code>function hasPassed(score) {
    return score >= 70;
}</code></pre>

            <h3>
                6. AI Chat
            </h3>

            <p>
                AI chat provides a conversational way to work with the
                coding assistant.
            </p>

            <p>
                You might ask:
            </p>

            <pre><code>Explain this file.

Why am I getting this error?

How can I make this function easier to understand?

Write tests for this function.

Explain the difference between let and const.</code></pre>

            <h3>
                7. AI Agents
            </h3>

            <p>
                More advanced AI coding systems may work in an agent-like
                way.
            </p>

            <p>
                Instead of producing only one answer, an AI agent may
                attempt several related development steps.
            </p>

            <p>
                For example:
            </p>

            <pre><code>Create a registration form,
add validation,
check the existing project structure,
and explain the files you changed.</code></pre>

            <p>
                This type of assistance can be powerful, but the developer
                should review important actions before trusting the final
                result.
            </p>

            <h3>
                8. GitHub Account and Sign-In
            </h3>

            <p>
                GitHub Copilot is connected to GitHub services.
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore, the developer normally needs to authenticate
                with a GitHub account before using Copilot features.
            </p>

            <p>
                Authentication means proving to the service which account
                you are using.
            </p>

            <p>
                A typical flow may look like:
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li>
                    Open Visual Studio Code.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Open the Copilot feature or extension.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Choose the sign-in option.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Authenticate using your GitHub account.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Return to the development environment.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Confirm that Copilot features are available.
                </li>
            </ol>

            <h3>
                9. Plans, Usage Limits, and Cost
            </h3>

            <p>
                AI coding services can have different access plans and
                usage limits.
            </p>

            <p>
                Some users may have limited access while heavier use may
                require a paid subscription.
            </p>

            <p>
                The exact names, prices, limits, and available features
                can change over time.
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore, an AI Developer should never build permanent
                assumptions around an old price or plan name.
            </p>

            <blockquote>
                <strong>
                    Always check the current official service information
                    before paying for an AI development tool.
                </strong>
            </blockquote>

            <h3>
                10. Usage Planning
            </h3>

            <p>
                Developers should think about how heavily they intend to
                use AI tools.
            </p>

            <p>
                Occasional code assistance may require less usage than
                running AI agents frequently across a large software
                project.
            </p>

            <p>
                This is one reason usage limits matter.
            </p>

        </section>

